WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
Prompt history should not be casually visible to every developer, administrator, analyst, vendor, or support user.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:14.520
The right access model depends on role, purpose, tenant, sensitivity, and need-to-know.

3
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:18.940
A support specialist may need to inspect a single user session with approval.

4
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.200
A security responder may need broader visibility during an investigation.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.060
A product analyst may need aggregated quality patterns without raw prompt content.

6
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.440
These are different privileges, not one generic admin permission.

7
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.760
Least privilege means granting the narrowest access that still supports the work.

8
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:48.580
Teams should separate duties for platform administration, customer support, security review, privacy review, dataset preparation, and product analytics.

9
00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:56.200
Elevated access should have a reason, a time limit where practical, and extra review for bulk access or sensitive tenants.

10
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:03.340
Break-glass access can be useful during urgent incidents, but it should be exceptional, monitored, and reviewed after use.

11
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.540
Dashboards and exports deserve special attention.

12
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.920
A dashboard that shows raw conversations may become a shadow data store.

13
00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.740
An export can move many records outside normal access controls and retention jobs.

14
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:26.880
Download permission, report sharing, API tokens, and service accounts should be treated as access to prompt history, not as harmless convenience features.

15
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.480
Audit trails make access accountable.

16
00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:45.180
The system should record who viewed, searched, exported, changed, deleted, or reused prompt records; when the action occurred; what scope was involved; and what business purpose or ticket justified the action when appropriate.

17
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:54.160
Regular access reviews help catch stale roles, inherited permissions, broad groups, and vendor accounts that no longer need access.

18
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:57.940
Strong governance is not only about who can open a record today.

19
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.800
It is also about proving that access stays limited over time.
